=head1 NAME

afsio_listacl - Display the ACLs of directories in AFS

=head1 SYNOPSIS

=for html
<div class="synopsis">

B<afsio listacl> S<<< B<-dir> <I<AFS-dirname>>+ >>>
    S<<< [B<-cell> <I<cellname>>] >>>
    [B<-clear>] [B<-crypt>] S<<< [B<-realm> <I<realm name>>] >>>
    S<<< [B<-as-user> <I<username>>] >>>  [B<-help>]

B<afsio fidlistacl> S<<< B<-vnode> <I<volume.vnode.uniquifier>>+ >>>
    S<<< [B<-cell> <I<cellname>>] >>>
    [B<-clear>] [B<-crypt>] S<<< [B<-realm> <I<realm name>>] >>>
    S<<< [B<-as-user> <I<username>>] >>>  [B<-help>]

=for html
</div>

=head1 DESCRIPTION

The B<afsio listacl> command displays the ACL of one or more directories
specified by the B<-dir> argument. The format is identical to that which is
used in B<fs listacl>, listing the normal rights first in user + rights pairs,
followed by the negative rights, repeated for every directory given.

The B<afsio fidlistacl> command performs the same operation, but identifies
the target file by its FID (the standard C<volume.vnode.uniquifier> triple)
specified in the B<-fid> argument.

=head1 OPTIONS

=over 4

=item B<-dir> <I<AFS-dirname>>+

Specifies each directory to list the ACL for. This option is used by
B<afsio listacl>. A path not beginning with F</afs> is interpreted relative to
the cell's F<root.cell>. A path beginning with F</> but not F</afs> is invalid.

=item B<-fid> <I<volume.vnode.uniquifier>>+

Specifies each directory to list the ACL of as a FID triple (e.g.,
C<536870915.1.1>). This option is used by B<afsio fidlistacl>.

=item B<-cell> <I<cell name>>

Specifies the AFS cell to operate in.

=item B<-realm> <I<realm name>>

Specifies the Kerberos realm to use for authentication.

=item B<-clear>

Uses unencrypted (cleartext) connections to file servers.

=item B<-crypt>

Uses encrypted connections to file servers.

=item B<-as-user> <I<username>>

Uses localauth credentials to pretend to be the given user, allowing
authenticated RXAFS operations without a running kernel client.

=item B<-help>

Prints the online help for this command. All other valid options are
ignored.

=back

=head1 EXAMPLES

The following lists the ACL of a directory:

   % afsio listacl test
   Access list for test is
   Normal rights:
     system:administrators rlidwka
     system:anyuser rl
     tester rlidwka

The following lists the ACL of two directories by FID:

   % afsio fidlistacl 536870915.3.827 536870915.1.1
   Access list for 536870915.3.827 is
   Normal rights:
     system:administrators rlidwka
     system:anyuser rl
     tester rlidwka

   Access list for 536870915.1.1 is
   Normal rights:
     system:administrators rlidwka
     system:anyuser rl
     tester rl

=head1 PRIVILEGE REQUIRED

The issuer must have the C<l> (lookup) permission on the directory in order to
be able to access the ACL.

The B<-as-user> option requires access to the local key material, which
is typically only readable by the local superuser C<root> on a server
machine.

